---
/**
 * The sequel to `/stacking/`. That page ends on an instruction — lower the correlation
 * rather than raise the count — and leaves the reader's obvious next question unanswered:
 * so what actually is uncorrelated, and how much is the whole exercise worth?
 *
 * The evidence behind this page is an outside review that shares no data with the
 * repository's own measurements, which is why the corroboration section exists and why it
 * is stated once rather than sprinkled. The numbers here are read from published sources
 * rather than measured here, so none of them carries a `<Figure>` record yet; they are
 * marked with `data-numeric` like the dated numbers on `/corrections/` and every one of
 * them is sourced in the block at the foot.
 */
import Breakout from "~/components/Breakout.astro";
import Callout from "~/components/Callout.astro";
import MarginNote from "~/components/MarginNote.astro";
import Rail from "~/components/Rail.astro";
import Base from "~/layouts/Base.astro";

/** The one-year odds of finishing ahead, by how many genuinely unrelated bets you hold. */
const ODDS_COLUMNS = ["Genuinely unrelated bets", "Chance of beating the index over a year"] as const;

const ODDS_ROWS = [
  { bets: "1", odds: "55%" },
  { bets: "5", odds: "61%" },
  { bets: "10", odds: "65%" },
  { bets: "20", odds: "71%" },
] as const;
---

<Base
  title="How many different bets you can buy"
  description="About four or five genuinely different sources of return exist for a normal investor, not twenty, and doing the whole thing well is worth roughly 1.5 to 2 percentage points a year."
>
  <div class="prose">
    <Rail>The answer</Rail>
    <h1 class="font-serif text-4xl tracking-[-0.02em] text-ink">How many different bets you can buy</h1>

    <MarginNote label="The question this answers">
      <p>
        If the instruction is to lower the correlation between my holdings, what is actually uncorrelated, and
        what is the finished job worth?
      </p>
    </MarginNote>

    <p>About four or five, and roughly <span data-numeric>1.5</span> to <span data-numeric>2</span> percentage
    points a year. Those are the two numbers. The rest of this page is where they come from and how much weight
    they will carry.</p>

    <p>The <a href="/stacking/">stacking page</a> tells you to lower the correlation between what you hold rather
    than add another fund. This is the question that follows it, answered from evidence outside this project.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="prose mt-14">
    <Rail>The odds</Rail>

    <h2>A 55% strategy is a weaker thing than it sounds</h2>

    <p>A strategy that beats a cheap index fund 55 times in a hundred sounds like a decent edge. Line up twenty
    of them, all genuinely unrelated, and charge yourself nothing in fees.</p>
  </div>

  <Breakout scroll class="my-8" label="Frictionless best case, one year">
    <table role="table" class="stack-table w-full border-collapse text-sm sm:min-w-[30rem]">
      <caption class="sr-only">
        The chance of finishing ahead of the index over one year, by the number of genuinely unrelated bets held
      </caption>
      <thead role="rowgroup">
        <tr role="row" class="border-b border-rule-strong text-left">
          {
            ODDS_COLUMNS.map((heading, i) => (
              <th role="columnheader" scope="col" class:list={["eyebrow py-2 pr-4", i === 1 && "text-right"]}>
                {heading}
              </th>
            ))
          }
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody role="rowgroup" class="text-ink-muted">
        {
          ODDS_ROWS.map((row) => (
            <tr role="row" class="border-b border-rule last:border-0">
              <th role="rowheader" scope="row" data-numeric class="py-2 pr-4 text-left font-normal">
                {row.bets}
              </th>
              <td role="cell" data-label={ODDS_COLUMNS[1]} data-numeric class="py-2 pr-4 text-right">
                {row.odds}
              </td>
            </tr>
          ))
        }
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </Breakout>

  <div class="prose mt-4">
    <p>Twenty unrelated bets, no fees, nothing shared between them, and you still have a
    <span data-numeric>29%</span> chance of trailing the index next year. That is the best case for a portfolio
    nobody can actually build.</p>

    <p>Now let those twenty share a little behaviour. At an average correlation of <span data-numeric>0.2</span>,
    which is lower than most real portfolios manage, twenty holdings behave like <span data-numeric>4.2</span> and
    the odds fall back to <span data-numeric>60%</span>. The ceiling from the stacking page, arriving by a
    different road.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="prose mt-14">
    <Rail>The live version</Rail>

    <h2>The best-built version of this idea delivered a quarter of the theory</h2>

    <p>AQR runs a fund that is the literal implementation of the stacking thesis. Dozens of separate premiums,
    four styles, spread across five groups of assets, built by the people who wrote the theory down and financed
    at institutional rates. If the idea works anywhere, it works here.</p>

    <MarginNote label="Return per unit of risk">
      <p>
        How much a holding paid for each unit of the bumpiness it put you through. A cheap global stock fund has
        run around <span data-numeric>0.4</span> to <span data-numeric>0.6</span> over long stretches.
      </p>
    </MarginNote>

    <p>Its independence is genuine. Measured against the S&amp;P 500 since it launched in October 2013, its
    exposure to the stock market is about <span data-numeric>−0.01</span>. So this is a fair test of the idea
    rather than a stock fund in a costume. The published forecast for a composite like that, from the firm's own
    head of research, was a return per unit of risk above <span data-numeric>2</span>. Over twelve and a half
    years it delivered <span data-numeric>0.46</span>, returning <span data-numeric>7.57%</span> a year while the
    S&amp;P 500 returned about <span data-numeric>13.5%</span>.</p>

    <p>The gap to the index looks damning and it is the wrong comparison. A stack like this is something you add
    on top of your stocks using borrowing, so its return arrives alongside the index rather than instead of it.
    Buy it by selling stocks and it loses to them, which is what the funding arithmetic on the stacking page says
    will happen. What the record settles is the size of the haircut. About four to one between the stacking
    mathematics and the delivered result, absorbed by fees, financing, and holdings less separate than the model
    assumed, and each of those is worse for you than for them.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="prose mt-14">
    <Rail>What is different</Rail>

    <h2>Four or five genuinely different sources exist, not twenty</h2>

    <p>Same firm, March 2026, its published assumptions for the decade ahead. Take the stock market exposure out
    of ten asset classes and ask what is left. Only three still have a meaningfully positive edge: commodities at
    <span data-numeric>0.24</span>, hedge funds at <span data-numeric>0.29</span>, managed futures at
    <span data-numeric>0.26</span>. High yield credit comes out at <span data-numeric>−0.06</span>, real estate at
    <span data-numeric>−0.03</span>, and private equity at <span data-numeric>0.06</span>, which is noise.</p>

    <p>Six of the ten correlate between <span data-numeric>0.68</span> and <span data-numeric>0.93</span> with
    stocks. And when the same firm pulled apart a typical eight-asset institutional portfolio, the kind held up
    everywhere as the diversified ideal, stocks alone accounted for <span data-numeric>81%</span> of its risk.
    Stocks plus credit, over <span data-numeric>90%</span>. Eight names on the pie chart, one and a bit bets.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="prose mt-14">
    <Rail>The prize</Rail>

    <h2>Doing all of it well is worth about two points a year</h2>

    <p>On the same firm's numbers, a well-built diversified portfolio is expected to return
    <span data-numeric>6.5%</span> to <span data-numeric>8.1%</span> a year at a return per unit of risk of
    <span data-numeric>0.26</span> to <span data-numeric>0.35</span>. Against the portfolio a normal person would
    otherwise be holding, the improvement is somewhere around <span data-numeric>1.5</span> to
    <span data-numeric>2</span> percentage points a year.</p>

    <p>On $100,000 that is $1,500 to $2,000 in the first year, and it compounds into a genuinely different
    retirement. So it is real money and worth having. It is also nowhere near "you will beat the market", and
    anybody selling it in those words is selling something else.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="prose mt-14">
    <Rail>The free one</Rail>

    <h2>The gain nobody sells you is worth about as much as all the rest</h2>

    <p>An exchange-traded fund can hand shares to a departing institution instead of selling them, so it realises
    fewer gains and passes fewer of them to you at the end of the year. Against equivalent mutual funds, that
    raised long-term investors' after-tax returns by <span data-numeric>1.05%</span> a year.</p>

    <p>On $100,000 in a taxable account that is about $1,050 a year, for no fee, no minimum and no skill. It is
    the same size as the entire tax case for direct indexing, which costs <span data-numeric>25</span> to
    <span data-numeric>40</span> basis points a year plus a good deal of paperwork. You have never been pitched
    it because nobody earns anything when you do it.</p>
  </div>

  <Callout kind="do-this" label="Worth ten minutes on your own account">
    <p>
      If you hold anything in a taxable account, check whether each fund there is an exchange-traded fund or a
      mutual fund. Inside a sheltered account it makes no difference. If one of them comes in both forms, the
      exchange-traded version is cheaper to own from here on. Check the tax bill on switching first, because the
      gain you would realise selling the old holding can be larger than several years of the saving.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <div class="prose mt-14">
    <Rail>The warning</Rail>

    <h2>Every one of these premiums shrinks when the money arrives</h2>

    <p>Merger arbitrage spreads have come down by more than <span data-numeric>400</span> basis points since
    2002. The roll return on commodities paid <span data-numeric>+1.95%</span> a year for the thirty-four years
    to 2004, then <span data-numeric>−9.29%</span> a year over the eleven years after index money showed up.
    Catastrophe bond spreads roughly halved, <span data-numeric>11.31%</span> to <span data-numeric>5.53%</span>,
    while the capital chasing them grew <span data-numeric>31%</span>. Published stock market factors decayed
    <span data-numeric>26%</span> to <span data-numeric>58%</span> once they were published.</p>

    <p>Five different premiums, one mechanism. Be most suspicious of whatever is being marketed hardest, because
    that is where the money is arriving. In 2026 that is return stacking and options income.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="prose mt-14">
    <Rail>Why we believe it</Rail>

    <h2>Two separate routes, one answer</h2>

    <p>One thing here matters more than any single number on the page. This project reached its conclusions from
    its own data: 422 months of returns, its own correlation measurements, its own arithmetic about how an
    addition gets paid for. The evidence above is an outside review of completely different material, which is
    manager research, SEC filings, Ken French's data library and FRED. It lands in the same place. Same ceiling,
    same count of separate engines, same size of prize.</p>

    <p>Neither route is strong alone. Ours is one portfolio over one window, and theirs is one firm's forecasts
    plus one fund's record. Two weak instruments that share no data and agree are worth more than either, and
    that is the closest thing to confirmation this site has.</p>
  </div>

  <Callout kind="caveat" label="How much weight these numbers carry">
    <p>
      Two of the four headline figures are forecasts rather than records. The count of different sources and the
      1.5 to 2 point prize both come from one manager's published assumptions about the next decade, and that
      manager sells products in the categories it is forecasting. The conflict runs against its own conclusion
      here, which is mild reassurance and not a control.
    </p>
    <p>
      The 0.46 is one fund over one stretch of history, so read it as evidence about the size of the shortfall
      rather than as a forecast of what that strategy earns. Nothing here was rebuilt from underlying data.
      Every number is a published figure, read on 2026-08-24.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <Callout kind="change-your-mind">
    <p>
      A market-neutral fund holding many premiums that reaches a ten-year return per unit of risk above 1 would
      move the shortfall above from four-to-one to something the theory can carry. So would a genuinely new
      source of return, cheap to buy, that correlates under 0.2 with stocks. The arithmetic on this page would
      not change. The ingredients would.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <div class="prose mt-14">
    <Rail>Sources</Rail>

    <h2>Where this comes from</h2>

    <MarginNote label="Standing caveats">
      <p>
        The author holds no licence, works for no firm and belongs to no professional body. AI was used heavily
        throughout. Backtests are hindsight with a chart attached.
      </p>
    </MarginNote>

    <p>Every figure on this page, with its period, its benchmark and what is thin about it, is set out in
    <a href="/research/how-many-independent-engines/">the outside evidence review</a>. The odds table and the
    correlation ceiling come from <a href="/research/stacking-and-effective-breadth/">the stacking
    measurements</a>, and why a financed addition beats a funded one comes from <a href="/research/capital-efficiency-and-breadth/">capital efficiency and breadth</a>. The
    exchange-traded fund result is Moussawi, Shen and Velthuis in the <em>Review of Financial Studies</em>, 2025,
    and direct indexing is priced against it <a href="/research/harvesting-and-direct-indexing/">on its own
    page</a>. <a href="/corrections/">Corrections</a> lists everything published here that later turned out to be
    wrong.</p>
  </div>
</Base>
